Student Employment Welty Health and Counseling Center Quick Links Bon Appetit Bookstore Campus Map Career Resources Communications Conferences & Events Getting to Whitman College Giving Human Resources Institutional Research Job Opportunities Penrose Library Security Technology Services Welty Health & Counseling Center WhitLife Whitman Stories Apply Visit Whitman Alumni Make a Gift Stories Events Internships Career Prep Career and Community Engagement Center Career Development Internships Student Internship Stories In the Career and Community Engagement Center , we believe in learning by doing. The Whitman Internship Grant (WIG) program provides students with invaluable hands-on experience across various industries, both in the U.S. and across the globe. In this series, we’ll share inspiring stories from students who have leveraged the WIG to explore work in tech startups, environmental organizations, art galleries, public health initiatives, and more. Discover how these internships are shaping students' futures and how you, too, can benefit from this incredible opportunity. Search Filter by Select an option All Topics Arts Education Engineering Environment and Sustainability Finance and Economics Government and Public Service Health and Medicine Law Marketing & Communications Politics and Activism Science Social and Human Services Technology Select an option All Years 2026 2025 2024 2023 2022 Select an option All Months January February March April May June July August September October November December Clear Filters Finding Patterns in Complex Biology Bassant Said ‘27 spent her summer conducting computational biology research at Johns Hopkins University. Through the internship, she explored how different types of data can be used together to investigate a complex and still poorly understood chronic pain condition. August 18, 2026 Prevention and Response with Local Public Health Finn Stukerjurgen ’29 spent his summer working at the Polk County Health Department in Des Moines, Iowa, for their Epidemiology and Emergency Preparedness teams. There he learned the skills and group dynamics to respond to public health emergencies and prevent them in the future. August 17, 2026 Communicating Local History at the Ol’ Fort Emmali Simmelink spent the summer as the Communications Intern at Fort Walla WallaMuseum, a local historical institution. She’s had many opportunities to learn about operations,media, and collections over the past few months/ August 17, 2026 Finding Freedom Under Pressure Charlie Boyden ‘27 spent his summer interning with world champion sports psychologist Jeff Greenwald at Fearless Tennis in Marin County, California. Through hands-on coaching and original research, Charlie discovered that the mental barriers holding athletes back are the same ones we all face in everyday life. August 17, 2026 Summer Spent Researching Rare Brain Cancer! Avery Riley ’28 first thought she was going to be a zookeeper, then an Olympian, then an FBI agent, but she has found her true passion after being awarded the opportunity to research cancer. She wants to continue to help people by discovering new ways to treat disease and understanding the mechanisms of illnesses that haunt our society. August 17, 2026 Exploring Nature with Kids A single mention of a day camp for kids on a local Walla Walla farm motivated Mairi Sessions ‘27 to ask the farm owner about their outdoor educator internship position. This summer, she led seven weeks of camps to encourage the campers’ confidence in the natural world and experience the impact of non-traditional learning spaces herself. August 17, 2026 Filling Elementary Schools With Creativity And Color Amy Charnholm ’27 recently had the opportunity to reach for new heights- quite literally- over twenty two feet in the air, elevated in a scissor lift when creating the largest mural yet of her portfolio, bringing her creative passions to local elementary schools in Independence, Oregon. August 17, 2026 What Research Taught Me About Health Care This summer, Brianna Garcia-Andrade ’27 gained hands-on research experience at an Oregon Health & Science laboratory studying barriers to health care for children living with autism. By interviewing participants, managing data and preparing survey mailings, she saw how research can shape better care. August 17, 2026 Uncovering Stories From the Numbers For Monyati Alsabbagh ’27, a corporate accounting internship has turned classroom concepts into real responsibilities. From posting residency invoices to analyzing purchasing trends, she is discovering how accounting can tell a story about an organization’s decisions and operations. August 17, 2026 Previous 1 2 3 4 5 … 12 Next 345 Boyer Ave. Walla Walla, WA 99362 General: 509-527-5111 Admission: 509-527-5176 Apply Visit Request Info Bias Reporting Grievance Policy Nondiscrimination Policy Right to Know Title IX & Sexual Misconduct Disability and Accessibility Privacy Policy Whitman Online Terms of Service © 2025 Whitman College. All Rights Reserved."
